Albert Gonzalez — A Developing Biography

From Underground Hacker to One of the Most Significant Cyber crime Cases in Modern History 

Albert Gonzalez is a former computer hacker and convicted cyber-criminal best known for his role in some of the largest payment-card data theft cases prosecuted in the United States.
Unlike security researchers who study systems to improve defenses, Gonzalez became known for using technical knowledge to compromise systems and steal financial information.
His story occupies a significant place in cybersecurity history because it represents the transition from traditional individual hacking toward organized, financially motivated cyber-crime.

The case involved:

underground cyber crime communities, stolen payment-card data markets, large-scale retail breaches, payment processor compromises,law enforcement infiltration operations,
and the evolution of modern cybersecurity defenses. Public records describe Gonzalez as the leader of hacking operations responsible for thefts involving tens of millions of payment-card numbers, including attacks involving retailers and payment-processing organizations. The U.S. Department of Justice described him as the leader of a hacking ring involved in massive identity theft cases and sentenced him to 20 years and one day in federal prison in 2010.

